DAY 22 – CLASSES AND OBJECTS

1. OBJECT-ORIENTED PROGRAMMING

Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming
paradigm that organizes a program around objects.

An object contains:

• Attributes → Data or properties
• Methods → Behavior or actions

OOP helps represent real-world entities in software
and makes programs easier to design, maintain and extend.


2. CLASSES

A class is a blueprint or template used to create objects.

It defines the attributes and methods that objects
created from the class can have.

A class is only a design until an object is created.


3. OBJECTS

An object is an instance of a class.

Objects are the real entities created from a class.

A single class can be used to create multiple objects.


4. ATTRIBUTES

Attributes are variables that store information
about a class or an object.

There are two main types:

• Class Attributes
• Instance Attributes


5. CLASS ATTRIBUTES

A class attribute belongs to the class itself.

It is shared by all objects created from that class.

A change in a class attribute can be reflected
in all objects unless an object overrides it.


6. INSTANCE ATTRIBUTES

An instance attribute belongs to a specific object.

Each object has its own values for instance attributes.

Instance attributes are usually created inside
the constructor.


7. METHODS

A method is a function defined inside a class.

Methods describe the behavior or actions that
an object can perform.


8. INSTANCE METHODS

An instance method works with a specific object.

It receives self as its first parameter.

self represents the current object that calls
the method.


9. CLASS METHODS

A class method works with class-level data.

It is defined using the @classmethod decorator.

The first parameter is cls, which refers to the class itself.

Class methods are generally used to access or modify
class attributes.


10. STATIC METHODS

A static method does not depend on either the object
or the class.

It is defined using the @staticmethod decorator.

Static methods are mainly used for utility or
helper operations.


11. SELF KEYWORD

self refers to the current object.

It is used inside instance methods to access
the object's attributes and methods.


12. CONSTRUCTORS

A constructor is used to initialize an object
when it is created.

In Python, __init__() is commonly used as the
constructor.

It is automatically called when an object is created.


13. OBJECT CREATION

Objects are created from a class.

Each object can have its own instance attributes
while sharing class-level attributes and methods
defined by the class.


14. ACCESSING ATTRIBUTES

Attributes can be accessed using the dot (.) operator.

General forms:

object_name.attribute_name

ClassName.attribute_name


15. ACCESSING METHODS

Methods are also accessed using the dot (.) operator.

Instance methods are generally accessed through
an object.

Class methods and static methods can be accessed
through the class.


16. FOUR PRINCIPLES OF OOP

The four main principles of Object-Oriented
Programming are:

• Encapsulation
• Abstraction
• Inheritance
• Polymorphism


17. ENCAPSULATION

Encapsulation combines data and methods into
a single unit.

It also helps protect data from unauthorized
access and accidental modification.


18. ABSTRACTION

Abstraction hides unnecessary implementation details
and exposes only the required functionality.

It allows users to focus on what an object does
without needing to know how it works internally.


19. INHERITANCE

Inheritance allows one class to acquire the
properties and methods of another class.

It promotes code reuse and reduces duplication.


20. POLYMORPHISM

Polymorphism allows the same method or interface
to perform different tasks depending on the object.

It makes applications more flexible and easier
to extend.


KEY TAKEAWAY

Class → Blueprint for creating objects.

Object → Instance of a class.

Attribute → Data or property.

Method → Behavior or action.

self → Refers to the current object.

__init__() → Used to initialize an object.

Class attribute → Shared by objects.

Instance attribute → Belongs to a specific object.

Instance method → Works with an object.

Class method → Works with class-level data.

Static method → Independent of object and class.

Encapsulation → Combines and protects data.

Abstraction → Hides implementation details.

Inheritance → Promotes code reuse.

Polymorphism → Same interface, different behavior.